  title="Desired state, one lock, and a short retry — Christopher Uzochukwu"
  description="How FleetPilot reconciles a simulated fleet, locks one instance with Postgres, and stops retrying on the fifth provider failure."
>
  <h1>Desired state, one lock, and a short retry</h1>
  <p class="dateline">Christopher Uzochukwu · 29 October 2026 · Africa/Lagos</p>
  <p>
    Public repository: <a href="https://github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot">github.com/chrisuzor/fleetpilot</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You and a housemate share a list on the counter. You look, see no rice, and add rice. They are
    holding an older copy, so they add rice too. Dinner is fine. The cupboard is not.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the shop is closed. You walk back. You walk back again. Four times, a little longer
    between each try, and the shutter is still down. A fifth walk does not open it. You put the
    pencil down, so that line on the list is free for someone to look at later.
  </p>
  <p>
    FleetPilot is that problem for a simulated fleet. The list you asked for sits in PostgreSQL.
    The list of what exists sits in a fake provider in the same process. Image, size, and region
    are labels on that provider. It does not boot a machine, and it does not call a cloud API. The
    rest of this page is how the code avoids the rice thing.
  </p>

  <h2>Reconcile writes jobs, and nothing else</h2>
  <p>
    A reconcile lists desired rows and lists the provider, then matches them by name. A name that
    is only in desired state becomes a create job. A name on both sides with a different spec
    becomes an update job. A name whose spec already matches is skipped. A name that exists only in
    the provider becomes a delete job.
  </p>
  <p>
    The reconciler does not call the provider’s create, update, or delete methods. Asking what
    differs is a different step from changing the provider. The queue keeps at most one pending or
    running job for a name. A second reconcile of the same difference does not insert another job.
    A job that is already running is left alone. If the pending job’s operation or spec changed,
    that job is updated and its attempt count returns to zero.
  </p>
  <p>
    The worker is what calls the provider. Create and delete are safe to repeat. If the name is
    already present, create returns success and does not call create again. If the name is already
    gone, delete returns success. An update whose spec already matches returns without calling
    update. The same checks apply when a running job is claimed again after 30 seconds without
    moving. A retry should not apply the same change twice.
  </p>

  <h2>One row lock per instance</h2>
  <p>
    Before the provider call, the worker locks that instance name. On PostgreSQL it inserts the
    name into <code>instance_locks</code> if the row is missing, then selects it with
    <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>. The transaction stays open for the provider call and ends
    when that call returns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Skip-locked is the decision. A second applier that finds the row already held does not wait. It
    gets no row, so it does not call the provider. The job goes back to pending, and the attempt
    count does not move. It is ready again after one poll, 50 ms with the default settings. Waiting
    on the row would hold a connection and a concurrency slot for work this process cannot do.
    Skipping frees that slot for a different name.
  </p>
  <p>
    Different names are different rows, so they can run together. The worker still bounds that. A
    channel of size 4 means at most four jobs are in flight in one process. Four is the default
    limit in the code, not a measured rate. Tests that do not start PostgreSQL use an in-memory
    lock with the same rule: a name that is already held does not run the provider call.
  </p>

  <h2>Retry four times, then stop</h2>
  <p>
    A provider error is not a lock miss. The worker counts it. The default budget is five attempts.
    After the first four failures the job returns to pending. The waits are 100 ms, 200 ms, 400 ms,
    and 800 ms: a 100 ms base, doubled after each failure, and never above 5 seconds. The fifth
    provider failure does not wait. The job is marked dead, and the name is free for a later
    reconcile to enqueue a new job if the two sides still differ.
  </p>
  <p>
    A held lock, or a cancelled context, is not one of those five. Those paths release the job and
    leave the attempt count where it was. The budget is for the provider failing, not for two
    workers colliding on the same name. Stopping at five keeps a permanent provider error from
    pinning that name forever. The next reconcile can see the difference again and start a new job.
  </p>

  <h2>A rolling update, not a canary</h2>
  <p>
    A rollout uses the same jobs. It takes desired names in name order, writes the new spec onto
    one instance, enqueues one update, and waits until that job succeeds. It then stores a health
    check. A healthy check moves to the next name. An unhealthy check pauses the rollout. A dead
    job pauses it as well and does not advance. There is no traffic split, and no subset is left
    on the old spec for comparison. That is a rolling update. It is not a canary. The API does not
    resume a paused rollout.
  </p>

  <h2>Who may ask</h2>
  <p>
    Routes under <code>/v1</code> read the <code>X-FleetPilot-Role</code> header. The value is
    <code>reader</code> or <code>operator</code>. A missing or unknown value is 401. A reader on an
    operator route is 403. Reconcile, instance writes, and starting a rollout require operator. A
    reader can list instances and read a stored health check and the audit log. The header is a
    label for local development. It is not a secret, and <code>GET /healthz</code> and
    <code>GET /metrics</code> do not read it.
  </p>
